NAHPU is more than a digital catalog app for natural history fieldwork. It is
an offline-first software system for recording, validating, exporting, and
backing up collection data at the point of collection.

Use this section when contributing to the main NAHPU app. The code docs are
split by topic so you can read only the part that matches your change.

## Topics

- [Setup](./setup) - Install the tools needed to build NAHPU locally.
- [Architecture](./architecture) - Understand how Flutter, Drift, Rust, and the
  NAHPU API crates fit together.
- [NAHPU API](./nahpu-api) - Learn where app code ends and shared Rust API
  crates begin.
- [Code conventions](./conventions) - Follow Dart, Flutter, Riverpod, and Rust
  conventions used in the app.
- [Database changes](./database) - Update Drift schemas and migrations safely.
- [Troubleshooting](./troubleshooting) - Fix common setup and build errors.
- [AI-assisted development](./ai-assisted-development) - Use AI tools
  responsibly while keeping human accountability for every contribution.

## Contribution flow

{% steps %}

1. Pick or open an issue in the [NAHPU repository](https://github.com/nahpu/nahpu/issues).
2. Fork and clone the repository.

   ```bash
   git clone https://github.com/nahpu/nahpu.git
   cd nahpu
   ```

3. Create a focused branch.

   ```bash
   git checkout -b your-branch-name
   ```

4. Make the change using the architecture and convention pages in this section.
5. Run the checks that match your change.

   ```bash
   flutter analyze
   flutter test
   python scripts.py frb --check
   ```

6. Open a pull request against the `dev` branch with a clear description,
   tests run, and screenshots for UI changes.

{% /steps %}

{% aside type="note" title="Rust logic lives outside the app" %}
The `rust/` directory in the app is a Flutter Rust Bridge wrapper layer. Core
Rust implementations live in NAHPU API crates such as `nahpu_db`,
`nahpu_export`, `nahpu_gis`, `nahpu_archive`, and `nahpu_configs`. See the
[NAHPU API](./nahpu-api) page before adding Rust behavior.
{% /aside %}
